Highly Efficient Electroconversion of Carbon Dioxide into Hydrocarbons by View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/C9SC0260 Cathodized Copper-organic Frameworks
Keywords: $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction; Copper-organic framework; Electrocatalyst, Hydrocarbon; Reconstruction.
The rapid growth of global population and industrial development require the explosive consumption of energy and fuels that leads to significant emission and accumulation of carbon dioxide $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}|)$ ,10o.1n03e9/oCf9StCh0e26m05aCin culprits for severe global warming and climate change.[1, 2] The natural photosynthesis is the only mean to balance the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ level, however, it has limited capability in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ utilization and conversion to maintain the crucial carbon cycle.[3] Therefore, developing efficient artificial $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation technologies are imperative to balance the carbon footprint and relieve the excessive $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emissions for sustainable human society.[4] Except for the physical storage, chemical conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to valuable chemicals have been investigated extensively.[5] Contrary to the thermochemical and photochemical approaches, electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ powered by renewable energy sources (solar, wind, etc.) is useful perspective due to their mild reaction conditions/systems and various valuable products.[6, 7] Extensive researches reveal that the carbon monoxide (CO) and formic acid (HCOOH) are the main products for electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion.[8-10] However, high energy density and value-added hydrocarbons, including methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ and ethylene $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{4})}$ are more desirable for further industrial practices.[11, 12] Copper $\mathrm{(Cu)}$ based nanomaterials show the potential capability to generate various hydrocarbons.[13-16] Nevertheless, these inorganic Cu compounds suffer from the low efficiency and selectivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion towards hydrocarbon production.[17-20] Recently, porous metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) constructed by the coordination bonds of organic ligands and metal ions attract extensive interest.[21] The adjustable molecular structures and designable metal categories make MOFs become the emerging alternatives for multidiscipline catalysis applications.[22, 23] Especially, the atomic level periodicity of Cu complexes allows the design of Cu active sites for potential $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electrolysis.[24-26] For example, Cu porphyrin molecules can convert $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with a Faradaic efficiency (FE) of ${\sim}30\%$ at $-0.98{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE,[23] while the Cu phthalocyanine shows the FE of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ of $66\%$ at $-1.06\mathrm{V}$ (vs. RHE).[25] Nevertheless, the crystalline Cu-MOFs itself show the limited efficiency on the electrochemical conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ products. For example, Cu(II) benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate (btc) MOF (HKUST-1) exhibits the production of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with a maximum Faradaic efficiency of $27\%$ and a partial current density of $4.4~\mathrm{mA~cm^{-2}}$ at $-1.16\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs. RHE),[25] while the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{CuTCPP})$ MOF demonstrate the foDrOIm: 1a0t.i10o3n9 Co9fSCf0o2r6m05aCte and acetate with a total current density of $4.5\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and total Faradaic efficiency of $85\%$ .[26] Another type Cu MOFs, named $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(a d e)_{2}(C H_{3}C O O)_{2}]}$ , demonstrate a little $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ with $0.7\%$ FE and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{5}O H}$ with $0.5\%$ FE for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion at $-1.55\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE.[27] Moreover, these Cu-based active materials often suffer from the structural evolution under the cathodic reductive environment but rarely reported.[25, 28] Therefore, developing suitable Cu-MOFs nanocatalysts and investigating their structure evolution would be significant for further understanding MOFs-based catalysts in highly selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion towards valuable hydrocarbon chemicals production.
In this work, Cu-ade MOFs are employed to investigate their structure and performance for the selective conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Interestingly, the Cu-ade nanosheets demonstrate an excellent catalytic performance towards hydrocarbon production with a total hydrocarbon Faradaic efficiency (FE) of over $73\%$ . Predominantly, ethylene $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{4})}$ with a maximum FE of $45\%$ is mainly produced at $-1.4\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE with a current density of $8.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , while methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ dominates at -1.6 V vs. RHE with an FE of $50\%$ and a current density of ${\sim}15$ mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Characterization results and electrochemical analysis reveal the reconstruction of cathodized Cu-MOFs accompanying with the formation of Cu nanocrystals. Along with the residual ligands, these active Cu crystals would be responsible for this excellent performance of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion towards hydrocarbon production. The contemporary approach towards structure evolution of CuMOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion to hydrocarbons would provide valuable understandings in developing efficient Cu-MOFs catalysts for selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electrolysis and beyond.
The Cu-ade MOFs nanosheets are constructed by coordinating $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions with adenine and acetic acid (Figure 1a).[29-31] The biomolecular adeninato/carboxylato ligands not only scaffold the abundant pores of Cuade MOFs but may also contribute protons to enhance $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electroreduction process due to the presence of Ncontaining functional groups.[32-35] Three Cu-ade MOFs with different thickness, named Cu-ade nanosheets (s-Cu-ade), nanoplate (p-Cu-ade) and nanocuboid (c-Cu-ade), are prepared by tuning tDhOeI: 1v0.o10l3u9/mC9eSCr0a2t6i0o5Cof methanol and water (Figure S1). X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) patterns of MOF products are consistent with metal-adeninate framework, which reveals the successful formation of crystalline Cu-ade MOFs (Figure 1b, S1).[36] Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) demonstrates the Cu-ade MOFs are in the width of $\sim500$ nm and length of several micrometers (Figure 1c). Notably, the transparent property suggests the thin nanosheet structure of Cu-ade MOFs. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image further approves the thin two-dimensional structure, and the transparent Cu-ade MOFs nanosheet show a porous structure with a smooth surface (Figure 1d).
The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electroreduction measurements of Cu-ade MOFs are performed in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KHCO3 electrolyte at the potential ranging from $-1.2\mathrm{~V~}$ to $-1.6\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE. Figure 2a demonstrates the potentialdepended current response in Ar- $\left\langle\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}_{2}\right.$ -saturated ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ solution. Compared with the current response in Arsaturated electrolyte, the significant enhancement of current density in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -saturated electrolyte implies the Cu-ade MOF catalysts may have the potential catalytic activity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. The gas products are monitored by two online gas chromatography (GC) systems (Figure S2), and the liquid products are analyzed by nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) technique (Figure S3). When the Cu-ade catalysts are applied in Arsaturated 0.1 M ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ solution, only $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ product is detected without any carbon-containing gas and liquid products such as CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . While in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KHCO3 solution, the major hydrocarbon products including $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ are detected by GC measurements and few liquid products such as HCOOH and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ are characterized by NMR analysis (Figure S3). These results suggest that Cu-MOFs are effective for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electrochemical reduction, whereas the reduction products are from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ rather than the carbon-containing Cu-ade catalyst system.
The constant potential test method is used to investigate the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction over three Cu-ade MOF catalysts (Figure S4). The detailed analysis of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electrochemical reduction shows that the main products of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction are hydrocarbons and hydrogen (Figure S5). The Faradaic efficiencies (FEs)DOoI:f10.C103HV9i4/eCwa9ASrntiCcdl0e2O6Cn0l2i5nCeH4 products over nanosheet structured Cu-ade MOFs are shown in Figure 2b. $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ is firstly produced at more positive potential than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , which means the electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ preferentially generate $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ product at lower potential. The total FE of hydrocarbons ( $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ ) keeps increase with more negative cathodic potentials and achieves the highest value of over $73\%$ at the potential of $-1.6\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE. Among which, the FE of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ increases firstly and then decreases while the FE of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ keeps increasing along with the increasing cathodic potentials. The maximum FE of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ can reach $45\%$ at $-1.4\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE, and the maximum FE of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is over $50\%$ at $-1.6\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE. This phenomenon is similar to other Cu-based electrocatalysts for hydrocarbon production by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electrolysis, as the larger potentials induce more difficult C-C coupling, thus resulting in decreased C2 products and increased C1 products.[37-39] The partial current densities of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ at different potentials are shown in Figure 2c. The partial current density of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ is 9.2 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $-1.5\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE, the high current density is rare on MOF-derived catalysts for converting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ .[23, 25-27] At the potential of - $.1.6\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE, the partial current density of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ achieves $15.0\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ accompanying with a total $73\%$ FE of hydrocarbons ( $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ ). Compared to the reported Cucomplexes, these cathodized Cu-MOFs show the excellent efficiency on the electrochemical conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ products (Table S1).
The electrolysis stability of Cu-ade MOFs nanosheet catalyst is further investigated at -1.4 V vs. RHE for 8 hours (Figure 2d). The total FE of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ is stable at ${\sim}60\%$ during the whole stability test. However, the total current density increases slightly, which might be due to some side reactions, such as hydrogen evolution and the gradual reduction of Cu-ade MOF nanosheet. The detailed FE evolution of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ are demonstrated in Figure S7d. With the prolong electrolysis, the FE of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ product decrease and the FE of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ increase as time go on. The similar trend is observed when the electrolysis is applied at relatively negative electrode potentials, the FE of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ is declining, and the FE of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is enhancing.
Among three Cu-ade MOFs, s-Cu-ade MOF nanosheets demonstrate the most signifiDcOaI:n1t0.1p0a39r/tCia9SlCc02u6r0r5eCnt density and highest FE for hydrocarbons. However, linear scan curves of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction over three Cu-ade MOFs are similar (Figure 3a). Very interestingly, the LSV curve of the s-Cu-ade in Ar-saturated electrolyte is in the middle, which implies the side hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) over the s-Cu-ade MOF may be inhibited. The lower FE of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ also indicates this phenomenon (Figure S5). The FE of both $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ are more than $50\%$ at $-1.4\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE (Figure 3b), and the partial hydrocarbons ( $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ ) current density of s-Cu-ade is also higher at relatively negative potential (Figure 3c). Other two Cu-ade materials show the same change rules in the FE of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . The FE of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ firstly increases to ${\sim}35\%$ at $-1.4\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE and then declines, and the FE of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ always increases with the increased potentials (Figure S6, 7). Among three Cu-ade MOFs, the performance of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to the hydrocarbons ( $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ ) is dependent on the thickness of Cu-ade MOFs, and the s-Cu-ade nanosheets demonstrate the best $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction performance (Figure 3, S5). Considering the same molecular structure and similar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction behaviors in the activity and selectivity (Figure S7), the electrochemical active area (ECSA) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements are further studied. To a certain extent, the ECSA represents the number of active sites determined by the electrochemical double layer capacitance (Figure S8). The capacitance is calculated by charging current density differences plotted against scan rates, p-Cu-ade, c-Cu-ade and s-Cuade are ${\sim}92~\upmu\mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , ${\sim}100\upmu\mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , ${\sim}138~\upmu\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively (Figure 3d) and the roughness factors $(\mathrm{R_{f}})$ are 4.6, 5.0 and 6.9, respectively. Thus, ECSAs for p-Cu-ade, c-Cu-ade and s-Cu-ade are $0.9\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , $0.98~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , and $1.34~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , respectively. The area-based activities of three samples demonstrates a similar trend in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction activity (Figure S9), indicating the enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction activity of Cu-ade MOF nanosheet is mainly ascribed to improved ECSA, which provide more active sites exposed to the reactant.
Furthermore, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements and the equivalent circuit for the Nyquist plots are displayed in Figure S9b. The EIS data is fitted by the internal resistance $(\mathsf{R}_{1})$ , the charge transfer resistance $(\mathrm{R_{s}})$ , and constant phase element (CPE 1and 2). Cu-ade MOF naDnOoI:s1h0.e10e3t9s Ce9xShCi0b26it05tChe smallest transfer resistance $\mathrm{(R_{ct})}$ , which implies the fast charge transfer during the electrochemical reaction process. The thin structure could contribute to sufficient contact of the reactant on nanosheets, which will result in a fast charge transfer over Cu-ade nanosheet catalyst to promote the hydrocarbon generation. Although the distribution of reduction product mainly depends on the microstructure and intrinsic active Cucomplex sites, obviously, the different morphology of Cu-ade MOFs determine the activity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Moreover, the thinner nanosheet with more active sites exposed would respond to the enhanced activity for hydrocarbon generation.
The excellent performance in hydrocarbons generation attracts our attention to the real active surface of Cu-ade MOFs. After the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electrolysis, the morphology Cu-ade MOFs demonstrate significant changes (Figure 4, S10). SEM observations show the morphology of initial Cu-ade MOFs nanosheets are destroyed (Figure 4a). Only the interconnected nanoparticles are present in the final cathodized MOFs. The corresponding TEM images of cathodized Cu-ade MOFs after $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ also indicate the nanoparticle aggregation (Figure 4b inset). High-resolution (HR) TEM image shows the inter-planar spacing of ${\sim}0.18~\mathrm{nm}$ and $0.21~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is related to the $\operatorname{Cu}(200)$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(111)$ facets, respectively (Figure 4b). XRD pattern of Cu-ade MOFs after the electrolysis also demonstrates that the crystalline structure is completely changed (Figure 4c), as the missing peaks for Cu-ade MOFs and the emergence of Cu crystal (JCPDS: 85-1326). The diffraction peaks located at 43.3o, $50.4^{\mathrm{o}}$ and $74.1^{\mathrm{o}}$ are attributed to Cu (111), Cu (200) and Cu (220), respectively (Figure 4c), which is consistent with the electronic microscopy observations (Figure 4b). Obviously, metallic Cu nanoparticles are formed in-situ by reducing Cu-ade MOF during the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electroreduction process. Other both Cu-ade MOFs also suffer similar morphologies and structure evolution (Figure S10).
Elemental distribution of the cathodized Cu-ade MOFs indicates that the cathodized MOFs demonstrate two distinct element distribution, one is copper enrichment region with segregated status (DFiOIg:u10r.1e03SV9i1/eCw19AaSrt,iCcl0be2)O6,n0lai5nCne d another is carbon enrichment region along with homogenous Cu element distribution (Figure S11c, d). For the Cu-rich area, it can be inferred that the Cu-ade coordination bond is broken from the Cu-ade MOF, and some Cu ion species are reduced to Cu cluster following the consequent segregation into copper nanoparticles. While for the C-rich area, it would be the organic ligands along with the residual Cu species. Nevertheless, the elemental mapping shows that other elements (C, O, and N) are uniformly distributed on the area of the entire cathodized sample, even the Cu-rich area (Figure S12). The surface of Cu-ade MOFs is characterized by XPS technique (Figure S13). Cu 2p XPS spectra of initial Cu-ade MOFs confirms the presence of $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ species (Figure 4d). After the cathodized $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electrolysis, the $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ satellite peaks disappear (Figure 4d), while the $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathsf{p}3/2$ and $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathrm{p}1/2$ belonging to $\mathrm{{Cu(0)/Cu(I)}}$ locate at 932.5 and $952.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 4d). Further Cu LMM auger peaks at ${\sim}570.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ and ${\sim}567.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ indicate the existence of $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ and $\operatorname{Cu}(0)$ (Figure S13). Moreover, from C1s and O1s XPS patterns of initial and cathodized s-Cu-ade MOF, the ratio of C-N peak decrease and the O-Cu peak disappear, which indicates the formation of metallic Cu in the cathodized Cu-ade MOFs nanosheets. The coordination environment of N species was analyzed by XPS results (Figure 4e). In the initial Cu-MOFs, N is binding with C and Cu to form the stable MOFs (Figure 4e). After the electrolysis, the N-C binding demonstrates a slight decrease, while the N-Cu bond is accordingly increasing (Figure 4e). This transformation suggests the splitting of N-C ligands and the enhanced N-containing ligands on the resultant Cu nanocrystals, which agrees well with the similar structure evolution of microscopy observation (Figure S12). Moreover, the Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) pattern also indicates the MOF structure evolution and the residual N-Cu and N-C bond (Figure 4f).[31] The peak appeared at ${\sim}2900~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is related to H-C-H, and enhanced peak at $\sim550~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ suggests the partial broken N-C and formed N-C in the cathodized Cu-ade MOF. Besides, the CuO nanosheet derived from s-Cu-ade is performed to catalyze $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. From the SEM and XRD results (Figure S14), the surface of inorganic $\mathrm{CuO}$ becomes rough after View Article annealing Cu-MOFs. The primary product of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction is $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at a relatively negDatOiI:v1e0 1p03o9t/eCn9StiCa0l2.60T5Che maximum FE of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ reach $32\%$ at $-1.6\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE. Therefore, this result, to a certain extent, proves that the as-formed Cu functionalized by the nitrogen contained organic ligands in the cathodized Cu-MOFs are essential for converting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ toward hydrocarbon generation.
In summary, cathodized Cu-ade-MOFs have been investigated for efficient electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction towards hydrocarbon generation. The cathodized $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{II}}}/$ ade-MOF nanosheets demonstrate excellent catalytic conversion towards hydrocarbon production with a total hydrocarbon Faradaic efficiency (FE) of over $73\%$ . Primarily, ethylene $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4})$ ) with a maximum FE of $45\%$ is mainly produced at $-1.4\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE with a partial current density of $8.5\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ , while methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ dominates at $-1.6\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE with a FE of $50\%$ and partial current density of ${\sim}15\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . It is found that Cu-ade MOFs suffer from the in-situ structure evolution under the reductive $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ environment. The reconstruction of cathodized CuII/ade-MOF and the formed Cu nanoparticles functionalized by the nitrogen contained ligands would contribute to the excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion performance. This work would provide more or less valuable insights and opportunities for the rational design of Cu-based MOF catalysts for highly efficient conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ towards hydrocarbon generation.
